<sn><md>### Types of Essential Elements

- Non-Mineral
- Mineral
- Quasi-Essential

**Total: 17 elements** are considered essential to plant growth.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 1. Non-Mineral Elements (Most Abundant)
- Carbon (C)
- Hydrogen (H)
- Oxygen (O)

`CHO`

*Note: These are not considered mineral nutrients but are the most abundant elements in plants.*
</md></sn><sn><md>#### 2. Mineral Nutrients (14 elements)
The remaining 14 essential elements are classified by their `relative abundance` in plants:

| Category | Definition | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| **Macronutrients** | Used in `relatively large amounts` (&gt;0.1% of dry plant tissue) | `OKaHaLowMaPotaNaiSafKa` Nitrogen (N), Phosphorus (P), Potassium (K), Calcium (Ca), Magnesium (Mg), Sulfur (S) |
| **Micronutrients** | Used in `smaller amounts` (&lt;0.1% of dry plant tissue) | `JiMaMoliboClock` Iron (Fe), Manganese (Mn), Zinc (Zn), Copper (Cu), Boron (B), Molybdenum (Mo), Chlorine (Cl), Nickel (Ni) |

&gt; **Important Note:** Micronutrients are often called "minor elements," but this does **not** mean they are `!less important` than macronutrients. Micronutrient deficiency or toxicity can reduce plant yield just as macronutrient deficiency or toxicity does.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Quasi-Essential / Beneficial Elements

These elements are:
- Required for **some** plants, but not all, to complete their life cycle.
- Capable of boosting plant `growth and productivity`.

**Examples:**

| Element | Role / Essential For |
|---|---|
| **Silicon (Si)** | Used in large amounts by most plants; considered a beneficial element. Proven essential only for `diatoms` and plants in the `Equisetaceae` family. |
| **Cobalt (Co)** | Proven essential only for `Leguminosae` when in symbiosis with nitrogen-fixing bacteria. |
| **Sodium (Na)** | Essential in small amounts for plants using the `C4 photosynthesis pathway` (mainly tropical grasses). |
</md></sn><sn><md>### Classification of Plant Nutrient

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Soil%20Fertility/assets/soil-nutrient-element-table.png)

Primary macronutrients are nutrients that the plant needs huge amounts of. Secondary macronutrients are still needed in large quantities, but not as much as the primary macronutrients.
</md></sn>